{{Infobox|title=Chiplet}}
<onlyinclude>A '''chiplet''' is an integrated-circuit die designed to operate as a component of a larger multi-die system. Chiplets partition computation, memory, or input/output functions across separately fabricated dies connected within a package; they may use different fabrication processes, but heterogeneity is not required.</onlyinclude>

== Architecture and integration ==
A chiplet system partitions a design into dies linked by package-level interconnects. Designers balance reuse, die area, manufacturing yield, inter-die bandwidth and latency, energy per transferred bit, power delivery, thermal coupling, assembly and test cost. Interfaces may be proprietary or use standards such as Universal Chiplet Interconnect Express (UCIe). An interface specification alone does not guarantee interoperability of arbitrary products.

== Packaging distinction ==
Chiplets can be placed side by side on a substrate or interposer, or vertically integrated. Thus ''chiplet'' describes modular die-level system architecture, whereas 2.5D and 3D describe integration arrangements; they are not synonyms.

